#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176906} :activity/id #uuid "10a44af1-1279-5145-b644-04f70ee20cb6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050219 :topic/id #uuid "371f9947-9795-5390-9732-f6b26485bd28" :topic/math-academy-id 3637} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177058 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable, and suppose that \n\n$$\nY = u(X),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $u(X)$ is a function of $X.$\n\nWe can use the distribution function method to compute the PDF of $Y.$ Broadly speaking, the distribution function method involves the following two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for $F_Y$ in terms of $F_X,$ where $F_Y$ and $F_X$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate $F_Y$ to get the probability density function $f_Y$ of $Y.$\n\nWe've already seen how we use the distribution function technique to find the PDF of $Y$ in cases where $u(X)$ is strictly monotonic (i.e., strictly increasing or decreasing). If $u(X)$ is monotonic, then there is a one-to-one correspondence between $Y$ and $X,$ so computing the PDF of $Y$ is reasonably straightforward.\n\nWe can also use the distribution function method in cases where $u$ is not monotonic, although a little more care is needed. This is what we'll focus on in this lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "5ea30d88-d8ee-5ddb-aae4-af9cb47a349d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6318 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a604edf3-7df8-5e22-b499-ffb860d66c1a" :step/math-academy-id 460559 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177062 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n![](images/13/13d7c25028403629b88e326a38ab2c8a7441bec64d52c5c0605b165867c864b5.svg)\n\nSuppose that we define a new random variable $Y$ as \n\n$$\nY = X^2-1.\n$$\n\n  \n\nFirst, notice that $Y = X^2-1$ is two-to-one over the interval $0 < |x| < 2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/e0/e0b5ff4184145d2b89ae0d334f452dd7603c95ee3d8e7fc6dc838bfeb1c2b5c1.png)\n\nMoreover, for $0 < |x| < 2,$ we have that $-1 \\lt y \\lt 3.$ \n\nLet's use the distribution function method to calculate the probability density function of $Y.$\n\nConsider the interval $-1 < y < 3.$ We write down the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ and then use our transformation to isolate $X$ in the parentheses. This will allow us to write $F_Y$ in terms of $F_X\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P({X}^{2}-1\\le y) \\\\  & =P({X}^{2}\\le y+1) \\\\  & =P(-(y+1)^{1/2}\\le X\\le (y+1)^{1/2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}((y+1)^{1/2})-{F}_{X}(-(y+1)^{1/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $-1 \\lt      y <          3,$ we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule, as usual. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+1)^{-1/2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}((y+1)^{1/2})+\\frac{1}{2}(y+1)^{-1/2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(-(y+1)^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+1)^{-1/2}\\cdot {f}_{X}((y+1)^{1/2})+\\frac{1}{2}(y+1)^{-1/2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(-(y+1)^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+1)^{-1/2}({f}_{X}((y+1)^{1/2})+{f}_{X}(-(y+1)^{1/2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X,$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+1)^{-1/2}({f}_{X}((y+1)^{1/2})+{f}_{X}(-(y+1)^{1/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+1)^{-1/2}(\\frac{3}{16}{((y+1)^{1/2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{16}{(-(y+1)^{1/2})}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y+1)^{-1/2}(\\frac{3}{16}(y+1)+\\frac{3}{16}(y+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}(y+1)^{-1/2}(y+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}(y+1)^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\sqrt{y+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n![](images/c7/c72cad36ae780f6de29b485e8636244b8ca7889de06ca287dccc0569b933c84a.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "84020d6b-58ad-532b-9d2c-4499dc6aa4e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6389 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "e208e03d-ea6e-50f4-95a2-3fa383623010" :step/math-academy-id 460560 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176908}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176072 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175850 :question/id #uuid "501ad713-9e9d-53d7-ac8e-397edd64bee1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9161"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f695215-606d-5c8b-87d2-dcf08bdc84e3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050525}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552499}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Two-to-One Transformations to Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "07b27e02-1644-59f5-a9a6-522391a1cb20" :step/math-academy-id 460561 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176909}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177059 :tutorial/content "We know how to compute the PDF of $Y = u(X)$ in cases where $u$ is either one-to-one or two-to-one over the entire support of $X.$ Let's now expand this to cases where $u(X)$ is both one-to-one and many-to-one over different intervals.\n\nAs an example, let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n![](images/e4/e4f9a33484de05dc9c341350536f80d3833f109f8cdae3a6618bb68ddaa019cf.svg)\nNow suppose that the random variable $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\nY = |X|.\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF of $Y,$ we separately apply the distribution function method on the regions where $Y$ is one-to-one and many-to-one.\n\nFirst, let's visualize the transformation $Y = |X|$ over the interval $x > - 1.$\n\n![](images/89/89a787d68543d136c0ab3951d70642e24d1759763928930fa3f948ba8ee3ddaa.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The transformation is one-to-one over the interval $x \\geq  1.$ On this interval, $y \\geq 1.$\n\n- The transformation is two-to-one over the interval $-1 \\lt            x \\lt            1.$ On this interval, $0 \\leq  y \\lt 1.$\n\nSo, we need to consider these two cases separately. \n\n- First, we consider the case $y \\geq 1.$ Using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $y \\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(|X|\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le y) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the fact that $|X| = X$ for $X > 0.$\n\nTo compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $y \\geq 1,$ we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({F}_{X}(y)) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}^{′}(y) \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X,$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{-(y+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the case $0 \\leq  y \\lt 1.$ Using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0 \\leq  y \\lt 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(|X|\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-y\\le X\\le y) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(y)-{F}_{X}(-y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $0 \\leq  y \\lt 1,$ we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({F}_{X}(y)-{F}_{X}(-y)) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}^{′}(y)+{F}_{X}^{′}(-y) \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(y)+{f}_{X}(-y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X,$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}(y)+{f}_{X}(-y) \\\\  & ={e}^{-(y+1)}+{e}^{-(-y+1)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(y+1)}+{e}^{y-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $y$ is\n![](images/a7/a796dd0706f9ab60f8f184c0243d63ea1aca373b66361c930259b45058b2f76a.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "bc3dea45-4a91-55b2-9b87-0a85daf732d0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6319 :tutorial/title "One-to-One and Many-to-One Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "8c8e89bb-adda-5755-8c13-451515bfd5e0" :step/math-academy-id 460564 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176910}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176910 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176073 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175690 :question/id #uuid "cd699f35-88bd-57b5-8413-f26ee0752f18" :question/math-academy-id "e-10130"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1034760-066d-5481-a644-ea198add71ec" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049636}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552524}] :knowledge-point/title "One-to-One and Many-to-One Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "df1c215e-19f9-5257-8808-c1abad8d8d03" :step/math-academy-id 460565}] :activity/title "The Distribution Function Method With Many-to-One Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176154}]}
